{
  "id": "proj-concrete-side-table",
  "slug": "concrete-side-table",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Concrete Side Table",
  "summary": "A cube table cast in a melamine form, with a recessed handhold. Heavy enough to never tip.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 8,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut the melamine into an 18 x 18 in base, two 15 x 15 in walls and two 16 1/2 x 15 in walls. Pre-drill and screw the walls to the base and to each other so the inside is a 15 in cube; screws only, so the form comes apart later.",
    "Run a thin bead of silicone along every inside seam and drag a fingertip down it to leave a small cove. Let it cure overnight: the coves become the table's rounded edges.",
    "Glue seven 12 x 12 in squares of 2 in foam board into a block with foam-safe adhesive. Glue a 5 x 1 1/4 x 1 in foam block to the inside of one wall, 2 in above the base, for the handhold. The form is cast upside down: the base becomes the tabletop.",
    "Wipe paste wax over every melamine and foam surface. Cut hardware cloth into a 14 in square for the top and four strips for the walls, so the mesh sits in the middle of the concrete.",
    "Mix Quikrete 5000 in the tub to a thick oatmeal consistency, wearing the respirator while the dry mix is open. Fill 1 1/2 in into the form and lay the top mesh on it.",
    "Centre the foam block on the wet layer and screw a melamine offcut across the top of the form to hold it down; foam floats. Fill the walls in 3 in lifts with the wall mesh in place, tapping the outside with the rubber mallet after each lift to bring air bubbles up.",
    "Screed the exposed rim flat, cover the form with plastic sheet, and leave it for two days. Unscrew the walls, pull out the foam, and keep the table covered for five more days. Expect about 120 lb.",
    "Wet-sand the faces with the diamond pads, rub a slurry of leftover mix into any pinholes, and let it dry for two days. Apply two coats of concrete sealer and stick felt pads on the bottom rim."
  ],
